  <h1>Auspicious 8 — Frequency Trainer</h1>
  <p class="sub">
    Learn frequency using the A8 second. The physical sound does not change; the time ruler used to count its cycles does.
  </p>

  <section class="panel native">
    <h2>What this trainer is teaching</h2>
    <p>
      <b>Primary:</b> frequency is displayed as <b>cycles per A8 second</b>.
      Conventional Hertz remains a secondary browser/reference translation because Web Audio still requires cycles per conventional second.
    </p>
    <div class="trainer-note">
      <div>
        <span class="k">A8 time ruler</span>
        <div class="big">1 A8 second</div>
        <div class="small">= 675/1024 conventional seconds</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="k">Native A8 frequency</span>
        <div class="big">512 cycles / A8s</div>
        <div class="small">A clean binary frequency value in the A8 ruler</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="k">Same physical tone</span>
        <div class="big">≈ 776.723 Hz</div>
        <div class="small">secondary legacy/browser reference only</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="small formula">
      Browser implementation: conventional Hz = A8 cycles/A8s × 1024/675.
      Reverse reference: A8 cycles/A8s = conventional Hz × 675/1024.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel native">
    <h2>Why the A8 ladder is naturally octave-friendly</h2>
    <p>
      A musical octave is a physical <b>2:1 frequency ratio</b>. A8 does not create that acoustic relationship;
      it represents it cleanly because binary doubling and halving are already native operations.
    </p>
    <div class="trainer-note">
      <div>
        <span class="k">One octave down</span>
        <div class="big">256 A8</div>
        <div class="small">exactly 1/2 of 512 A8</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="k">Trainer anchor</span>
        <div class="big">512 A8</div>
        <div class="small">reference point for octave distance</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="k">One octave up</span>
        <div class="big">1024 A8</div>
        <div class="small">exactly 2× 512 A8</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
      The <b>2:1 octave ratio is the physical/acoustic fact</b>. The unusually clean sequence
      256 → 512 → 1024 is the A8 representation of that fact. This is “octave-friendly by structure,”
      not a claim that Nature itself uses a human numeral system.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <div class="controls">
